BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:waag-trust-me-im-an-airtist-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115743Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trust me\, I'm an AI/rtist: Open Call for Creative Interv
 entions
DESCRIPTION:Waag Futurelab invites artists\, theatre makers\, choreographer
 s\, dancers\, game designers and other creative practitioners to engage di
 rectly with seven experimental generative AI Enablers being co-created wit
 h technologists and dance\, theatre and gaming professionals. The call is 
 an invitation into the development process: selected artists get access to
  the technologies\, datasets\, interfaces and assumptions that shape these
  systems while they are still being built. Rather than testing whether the
  systems work\, artists are asked to interrogate why they work the way the
 y do\, who they are designed for\, what forms of creativity they enable or
  constrain\, and what assumptions about culture\, intelligence\, authorshi
 p\, labour\, bodies\, audiences and society are embedded in them. Waag is 
 looking for individuals and collectives willing to move beyond adoption in
 to investigation\, and welcomes proposals that are speculative\, critical\
 , poetic\, provocative\, humorous\, performative\, constructive or disrupt
 ive\, including work that challenges\, misuses\, reimagines\, hacks\, subv
 erts or plays with the tools\, exposes hidden biases\, questions environme
 ntal costs\, or imagines alternative technological futures. THE SEVEN AI E
 NABLERS\, each requiring an on-site visit to its technology partner: the G
 eneric Procedural Content Generation Enabler (text-to-3D assets and avatar
 s\, CERTH\, Greece)\; the Data-Driven Cultural Content Adaptation Enabler 
 (audience-aware creative content ideation with ranked candidate ideas\, CE
 RTH\, Greece)\; the Cross-Domain Audience Engagement Analytics Enabler (tu
 rning heterogeneous cultural participation data into interpretable engagem
 ent signals\, The Audience Agency\, Warrington\, UK)\; the Dance Style Ana
 lysis and Generation Enabler (dance video into virtual 3D choreography\, d
 issected into body\, effort\, shape and space components\, Centre of Excel
 lence for Research & Innovation\, Cyprus)\; the Immersive Design Integrati
 on Enabler for Dance & Theatre (NLP-driven XR environments with speech-to-
 3D scene editing\, Cyprus)\; the Music-to-Movement Translation Enabler (au
 dio into a time-aligned feature set spanning genre classification\, emotio
 nal valence-arousal analysis\, rhythm extraction and latent-space projecti
 on\, feeding motion generation\, spatial audio and visual choreography\, A
 ristotle University of Thessaloniki\, Greece)\; and the Adaptive Narrative
  Enabler for Games & Theatre (an evolutionary AI trained on creator-writte
 n story elements that generates infinite story variations\, explicitly rej
 ecting LLMs because of their dependence on copyrighted or public-domain co
 rpora that encode 19th and early 20th century sexism\, racism and imperial
 ism\, CAUSA Creation\, Austria). ELIGIBILITY: artists\, designers\, creati
 ve technologists\, theatre makers\, dancers\, choreographers and game desi
 gners\, as individuals or collectives\; applicants must hold a European VA
 T number. An info session with live demonstrations of the AI Enablers runs
  31 July 2026\, 10:00 to 11:30 CEST (meeting ID 995 7016 2341). Applicatio
 ns go through Homerun using the official Application Template\, uploaded a
 s a PDF\; read the Guide for Applicants\, which contains the full Enabler 
 descriptions\, before applying. Questions: zoenie at waag dot org. The AI 
 Enablers and this call are part of the Horizon Europe project HAMLET\, whi
 ch aims to make generative AI accessible to cultural organisations of all 
 sizes. Closes 30 September 2026\, 23:59 CEST. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Waag Futurelab (Horizon Europe project HAMLET)\nLocation: Europe. Enga
 ging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site visit to its technolog
 y partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warrington\, UK)\, the Ce
 ntre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\, Aristotle Universi
 ty of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austria). Final presentat
 ions September 2027.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 commission fee excluding VAT\, in
 clusive of artist fee\, travel\, accommodation and production costs. Appli
 cants must have a VAT number in Europe. No application fee.\nDuration: Jan
 uary to September 2027\, with final presentations in September 2027\n\nMor
 e: https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe. Engaging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site vi
 sit to its technology partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warri
 ngton\, UK)\, the Centre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\
 , Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austr
 ia). Final presentations September 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mats-residency-program-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115743Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: MATS Residency Program: 6-24 Month Funded AI Safety Resea
 rch (Winter 2027 intake)
DESCRIPTION:The MATS Residency\, announced in August 2026 as a new track al
 ongside the seasonal MATS fellowship\, funds 6 to 24 months of high-indepe
 ndence AI safety research for experienced researchers\, whether already in
  AI safety or moving in from adjacent fields. COMPENSATION: USD 155\,000 t
 o 285\,000 per year gross depending on location\, seniority and experience
 \, plus insurance\, retirement plan\, PTO (17 days US / 20 days UK)\, conf
 erence funding\, catered meals\, and up to USD 25\,000 in relocation suppo
 rt. ELIGIBILITY: at least one substantial piece of research-caliber work\;
  credentials are not required. Full-time (part-time case by case for acade
 mics on sabbatical)\, in person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or 
 Washington DC\, with visa support where required. TIMELINE: the Winter 202
 7 intake window runs 25 August to 31 October 2026 (AoE)\, with decisions b
 y end of November and interviews plus a paid work test in the first week o
 f December\; three intake windows per year.\n\nOrganisation: MATS (ML Alig
 nment and Theory Scholars)\nLocation: In-person at MATS offices in Berkele
 y\, London\, or Washington DC\; visa support provided where required. For 
 experienced researchers with at least one substantial piece of research-ca
 liber work.\nAward: USD 155\,000 to 285\,000 per year gross (set by locati
 on\, seniority\, and experience)\, plus medical/dental/vision/life insuran
 ce\, retirement plan\, paid time off\, conference funding\, catered meals\
 , and up to USD 25\,000 relocation support. No application fee.\nDuration:
  6 to 24 months with rolling start dates\; three intake windows per year.\
 n\nMore: https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In-person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or Washington DC\
 ; visa support provided where required. For experienced researchers with a
 t least one substantial piece of research-caliber work.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:braid-creative-commissions-a-promise-in-return-2027@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115743Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261102
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261103
SUMMARY:Deadline: BRAID Creative Commissions 2027: A Promise in Return (Res
 ponsible AI\, GBP 8\,000-12\,000\, Edinburgh Art Festival)
DESCRIPTION:BRAID\, the UKRI AHRC-funded Responsible AI programme at the Un
 iversity of Edinburgh\, is commissioning around seven creative works that 
 treat Responsible AI as a question of relationships\, commitments and obli
 gations rather than a technical checklist or a set of rules\, under the ti
 tle A Promise in Return. WHO: UK-based creative practitioners in any mediu
 m: artists\, designers\, musicians and performers\, architects and urban d
 esigners\, craftspeople and artisans\, writers and poets. Full-time underg
 raduate and taught postgraduate students are not eligible\; PhD students m
 ay apply if registered as self-employed freelancers. WHAT YOU GET: a commi
 ssion budget of GBP 8\,000 to 12\,000\, production support from Inspace an
 d the Institute for Design Informatics\, and presentation during the Edinb
 urgh Art Festival in August 2027 with the Edinburgh Futures Institute. APP
 LICATION: online form (written or an eight-minute video application) plus 
 a single PDF of portfolio\, CV and budget emailed to braid@ed.ac.uk\; late
  submissions are not accepted. DEADLINE: Monday 2 November 2026 at 17:00 G
 MT.\n\nOrganisation: BRAID (Bridging Responsible AI Divides)\, University 
 of Edinburgh\, with Inspace\, the Institute for Design Informatics and the
  Edinburgh Futures Institute\nLocation: UK. For UK-based creative practiti
 oners\; work is presented in Edinburgh during the Edinburgh Art Festival\,
  August 2027.\nAward: Around seven commissions with budgets of GBP 8\,000 
 to 12\,000 each\, inclusive of VAT and all costs. No application fee.\nDur
 ation: Projects run January to August 2027\, presented during the Edinburg
 h Art Festival in August 2027\, with possible further displays through 202
 8.\n\nMore: https://braiduk.org/creative-commissions?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://braiduk.org/creative-commissions?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:UK. For UK-based creative practitioners\; work is presented in Edi
 nburgh during the Edinburgh Art Festival\, August 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:daiwa-foundation-small-grants-march-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115743Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270331
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270401
SUMMARY:Deadline: Daiwa Foundation Small Grants\, March 2027 round (GBP 2\,
 000-9\,000 for UK-Japan projects)
DESCRIPTION:The Daiwa Foundation Small Grants support projects that promote
  interaction between the UK and Japan in any field\, including arts exchan
 ges\, exhibitions\, conferences\, grassroots and educational exchanges and
  research travel\, with travel between the two countries as the main focus
 . New initiatives are encouraged\, and gender equality and SDG themes are 
 welcome\; continuing or regular events (such as an annual festival edition
 ) are unlikely to be funded without a strong reason or special occasion. N
 OT ELIGIBLE: commissions of artworks\, capital costs\, consumables\, salar
 ies or professional fees\, tuition and commercial activity. WHAT YOU GET: 
 GBP 2\,000 to 9\,000 (most awards GBP 3\,000-4\,000)\, with flights funded
  up to GBP 1\,200 each and subsistence up to GBP 100 per person per day\, 
 plus GBP 50 extra per flight for environmental projects in Japan. APPLICAT
 ION: read the Application Notes\, then apply via the Foundation's online p
 ortal (separate forms for UK- and Japan-based applicants)\; advice from gr
 ants@dajf.org.uk. Decisions by late May or early June 2027. DEADLINE: 31 M
 arch 2027 (the following round closes 30 September).\n\nOrganisation: Daiw
 a Anglo-Japanese Foundation\nLocation: For individuals\, societies\, assoc
 iations and other bodies in the UK or Japan running projects that connect 
 the two countries.\nAward: GBP 2\,000 to 9\,000 (typical awards GBP 3\,000
 -4\,000)\; flights up to GBP 1\,200 each and subsistence up to GBP 100 per
  person per day. A contribution\, not full funding. No application fee.\nD
 uration: Per project\; activity can start from late May or early June 2027
 .\n\nMore: https://dajf.org.uk/grants-awards-and-prizes/daiwa-foundation-s
 mall-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://dajf.org.uk/grants-awards-and-prizes/daiwa-foundation-small-gra
 nts?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:For individuals\, societies\, associations and other bodies in the
  UK or Japan running projects that connect the two countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:costar-southbank-creative-intelligence-award-2027@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115743Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261217
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261218
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Creative Intelligence Award 2027 (CoSTAR National Lab
  x Southbank Centre)
DESCRIPTION:A new co-commission from CoSTAR National Lab and Southbank Cent
 re to take a creative technology idea that is ready to be completed\, hone
 d or technically advanced through focused R&D\, then present it to audienc
 es at Southbank Centre's Creative Intelligence festival in autumn 2027. Th
 ey want original concepts where AI\, immersive\, real-time\, virtual produ
 ction or other emerging technology is integral rather than an add-on\, acr
 oss any artform (music\, theatre\, dance\, literature\, visual and live ar
 t\, design\, architecture\, food\, film\, games)\, with cross-disciplinary
  work encouraged and touring potential valued. ELIGIBILITY: lead applicant
  must be a UK-registered\, trading company or other eligible UK legal enti
 ty\; consortia welcome\, and artists\, technologists\, researchers and sol
 e traders can take part as collaborators. Team and funded activity must be
  UK-based. Webinar Q&A on 16 November 2026\, 14:00 GMT. DEADLINE: 17 Decem
 ber 2026\, 14:00 GMT.\n\nOrganisation: CoSTAR National Lab and Southbank C
 entre\, supported by UKRI\nLocation: UK (lead applicant must be a UK-regis
 tered company\; project activity UK-based). Prototyping at Pinewood Studio
 s\; showcase at Southbank Centre\, London.\nAward: GBP 50\,000 grant (one 
 award)\, plus up to GBP 25\,000 in AI compute and cloud credits\, 2 x 5 da
 ys studio access at Pinewood Studios\, a six-week CoSTAR prototyping team 
 sprint\, 3 days pre-show testing\, Southbank curatorial and producing supp
 ort\, and 20 hours of mentorship. No application fee.\nDuration: R&D devel
 opment through 2027\, presented at Creative Intelligence festival\, Southb
 ank Centre\, autumn 2027\n\nMore: https://www.costarnetwork.co.uk/calls/th
 e-creative-intelligence-award?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://www.costarnetwork.co.uk/calls/the-creative-intelligence-award?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:UK (lead applicant must be a UK-registered company\; project activ
 ity UK-based). Prototyping at Pinewood Studios\; showcase at Southbank Cen
 tre\, London.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
